module lowerer
  source: ["lowerer.ly"]
  why: "Translates the type-checked AST into LIR (LProgram). Flattens nested expressions into SSA-like temp-def chains, resolves type sugar to LType, lowers enums to tagged unions, classes to heap handles, try (?) to error-extract-and-early-return, and short-circuit logical ops to if-statements — so backends never see nested expressions."

  doc "Architecture":
    """
    The lowerer converts a type-checked AST into an LProgram via two phases:

    1. register_block: collect all struct/class/enum/interface/function
       declarations into Dict registries (no code generation). Populates
       variant tags, class fields, class field defaults, enum variants,
       impl-method renames, type aliases, and import aliases.

    2. lower_file: walk all blocks, lower each declaration to LIR. Functions
       are lowered by walking body statements, flattening expressions into
       temp chains via lower_expr → emit_temp.

    Key transforms:
    - Enums → LTyTaggedUnion; match → switch on variant tag.
    - Classes → LTyClassHandle (heap pointer); field access via class_get/set.
    - Try (?) → extract error → null check → early return → extract value.
    - Generators → goroutine + channel pattern.
    - Lambdas → LExprFuncLit with params and body.
    - String interpolation → LExprFormat with parts list.
    - Impl blocks → forwarding wrapper methods.
    - The Lyric `error` interface → LTyErrorResult (T, error) tuples;
      ErrorResult fields `._0`/`._1` map to `.value`/`.error` in C.

    Phase 1 MUST complete across ALL blocks before Phase 2 begins, so
    forward references and cross-file references resolve correctly
    (same pattern as the checker).
    """

  invariant "Two-Phase Architecture":
    procedural
    """
    The lowerer uses two phases, matching the Go implementation:
    1. register_block: collect all struct/class/enum/interface/function
       declarations into Dict registries (no code generation).
    2. lower_file: walk all blocks, lower declarations to LIR.

    Phase 1 MUST run across ALL blocks before Phase 2 begins. This
    ensures forward references and cross-file references resolve
    correctly (same invariant as the checker's phase ordering).
    """

  invariant "Type Lowering Fallback":
    procedural
    """
    lower_type() resolves AST TypeExpr to LType. For identifier types it
    checks, in order: primitives → structs → classes → enums → type aliases.
    If nothing matches, it assumes LTyTypeVar (generic type parameter).

    Consequence: typos in type names silently become type variables. The
    checker is responsible for catching these, but has gaps during bootstrap
    — when generated C produces void* for a "generic" parameter, suspect a
    typo upstream.
    """

  invariant "Enum Variant Disambiguation":
    procedural
    """
    Multiple enums may share variant names (e.g. Ident exists in ExprKind,
    PatternKind, and TypeExprKind). The lowerer MUST consult the checker's
    type annotation on each expression to determine which enum a variant
    belongs to. Without this, last-writer-wins in the variant lookup map
    causes wrong enum variant construction.

    Three levels of disambiguation:
    1. lower_call/lower_ident: check resolved_type to pick the correct enum.
    2. Match lowering: use enum-specific variant maps keyed by enum name.
    3. Variant constructor: inherit field types from enum declaration.
    """

  invariant "Error Type Resolution":
    procedural
    """
    The Lyric `error` interface maps to LTyErrorResult for (T, error)
    tuples. In the Go compiler the checker types `error` as TyInterface;
    the lowerer MUST detect TyInterface with name "error" and remap it to
    LTyErrorResult.

    ErrorResult field layout: `._0` → `.value` (the T), `._1` → `.error`
    (const char*). Return of `(T, error)` tuples is wrapped with
    `lyric_ok` / `lyric_err` in the emitted C.
    """

  invariant "Expression Flattening":
    procedural
    """
    Every lower_expr call emits LStmtTempDef statements and returns an
    LValue. Backends never see nested expressions — they see a sequence
    of temp definitions plus a final LValue reference.

    The temp counter (temp_id / next_temp) is per-function and MUST be
    reset at the start of lower_func; otherwise temp names collide
    across functions in the same program.
    """

  invariant "Class Field Defaults":
    procedural
    """
    Class fields can declare default values. The lowerer stores these in
    class_defaults during register_block. During struct-literal lowering
    for class allocations, any field not explicitly provided in the
    literal MUST be filled in from class_defaults — otherwise the
    constructed object has uninitialized fields.
    """

  invariant "Alias-Wrapper Bodies Bypass Rename Map":
    procedural
    """
    Alias-form impl mappings (`G.nodes = MyGraph.nodes`) cause
    lower_impl_block to synthesize a forwarding wrapper named
    `IfaceName_methodName` (e.g. `DiGraph_nodes`) on the concrete class,
    AND to register `class_name + "." + method_name` → wrapper in
    impl_method_renames so external call sites route through it.

    The wrapper BODY itself MUST NOT use ExMethodCall on self — that
    lowering re-consults impl_method_renames, and for the common case
    where target_member == method_name the call points right back at
    the wrapper, producing infinite runtime recursion. The wrapper
    body emits an ExCall directly to the user-helper's mangled C name
    (`target_class + "_" + target_member`), bypassing the rename
    machinery entirely.

    Discovered via 4w1-a default-method emission probe (2026-06-23):
    the bug was dormant for as long as no call site reached the
    wrapper at runtime; the desugar specialize pass made wrappers
    reachable and exposed it.
    """

  invariant "Short-Circuit Lowering":
    procedural
    """
    Logical `&&` and `||` MUST NOT be lowered as plain binary ops — that
    would force eager evaluation of the right operand and segfault on
    null-guarded code like `p != null && p.field == 0`.

    They are lowered to if-statements instead:
      `a && b` → `let mut t = a; if t { t = b }`
      `a || b` → `let mut t = a; if !t { t = b }`
    """

  invariant "Statement Buffer Management":
    procedural
    """
    The lowerer uses save_stmts() / restore_stmts() to temporarily
    redirect emitted statements into a sub-buffer (e.g. for if/else
    bodies, while conditions, match arms). The previous buffer MUST be
    restored after the nested processing completes.

    This is analogous to the checker's push_scope() / pop_scope()
    pattern: every save_stmts() MUST be paired with a restore_stmts()
    on every control-flow path.
    """

  invariant "Special LValue Kinds for Mutation":
    procedural
    """
    Most expressions flatten to ValTemp, but mutation and reference
    semantics require specialized LValue kinds:

    - ValIndexRef: used for index expressions passed as mutable arguments
      (e.g. `slice[i]` as `&mut`). Allows the backend to emit direct
      pointer references (`&collection.data[index]`) instead of copying
      to a temp.

    - ValClassFieldRef: used when calling mutating methods (push, pop)
      on slice headers stored within class fields. The backend MUST
      mutate the actual field rather than a temporary copy of the slice
      header, since slice headers contain (ptr, len, cap) and mutations
      update len/cap in place.

    Failure to use these specialized kinds causes silent data loss:
    mutations apply to a temporary copy that is immediately discarded.
    """

  invariant "Builtin and Method Specialization":
    procedural
    """
    The lowerer specializes many high-level method calls into LIR
    builtins (ExBuiltin). String, slice, and map methods — len, push,
    append, contains, delete, keys — are translated to dedicated
    builtin expressions rather than generic method calls.

    Critical pattern for `append` on class fields: when a slice field
    is appended to (e.g. `self.items.append(x)`), the lowerer MUST:
    1. Emit ExClassGet to read the current slice header from the field.
    2. Perform the append operation on the header.
    3. Emit StClassSet to write the updated header back to the field.

    Without step 3, the updated (ptr, len, cap) from append is lost
    because Go/C slice headers are value types — append may reallocate,
    returning a new header that must be stored back.
    """

  invariant "Pattern Matching Strategy Selection":
    procedural
    """
    The lowerer analyzes match arms to select the most efficient
    lowering strategy:

    1. Enum match → StSwitch on the variant tag (integer dispatch).
    2. Union/any match → StTypeSwitch on runtime type (type dispatch).
    3. Nested variant patterns → nested switch statements with value
       extraction and binding at each level.
    4. Simple literal or identity → StIf chains (sequential comparison).

    Strategy selection is determined by the match value's type:
    - LTyTaggedUnion → enum match path
    - LTyAny or union types → union type-switch path
    - Otherwise → if-else chain fallback

    Nested enum patterns (e.g. `Some(Ok(val))`) require multi-level
    switch emission via emit_nested_enum_match, which recursively
    extracts inner variants.
    """

  invariant "Concurrency Primitive Lowering":
    procedural
    """
    The lowerer translates Lyric's high-level concurrency primitives
    into flat LIR operations:

    - spawn blocks → StSpawn with an LSpawnData structure.
    - select statements → decomposed into [LSelectCase] structures
      with per-case channel operations.
    - Channel send/receive → dedicated LIR statements.
    - ref/unref → StRefIncr / StRefDecr for explicit reference
      counting (backend emits retain/release calls).
    - lock blocks → StLock statements ensuring thread-safe access
      to shared resources.

    Note: the `captures` list in LSpawnData is currently empty —
    proper capture analysis is not yet implemented (see Technical
    Debt). Variables from the outer scope used within spawn blocks
    are not explicitly tracked.
    """

  invariant "Variable and Assignment Lowering":
    procedural
    """
    Variable declarations lower to StVarDecl with support for
    multi-variable destructuring (e.g. `let (a, b) = pair()`).

    Type propagation rule: when a variable is declared with an
    explicit type and initialized with an empty or untyped slice
    literal, the lowerer MUST propagate the declared type to the
    slice initializer. Without this, the LIR emits a void* slice
    that causes C type errors downstream.

    Assignment lowering (lower_assign) distinguishes:
    1. Simple identity assignment → StAssign to the variable.
    2. Field access → StStructSet (structs) or StClassSet (classes).
    3. Index-based → StIndexSet.
    4. Compound pattern `slice[i].field = val` → StIndexSet with a
       field name parameter, avoiding an unnecessary copy of the
       element to a temp before writing.
    """

  doc "Interface and Impl Lowering":
    """
    The lowerer handles `impl` blocks through lower_impl_block, which
    generates forwarding wrapper functions for interface method
    implementations. Lyric's relations system allows classes to
    implement interfaces through several mapping forms:

    - Alias mapping: `G.nodes = MyGraph.nodes` generates a wrapper
      that forwards the interface call to the concrete method.
    - Inline function: `G.nodes = fn(self) -> [Node] { ... }` emits
      the function body directly.
    - Field binding: `G.name = MyGraph.name` generates getter (and
      optionally setter) wrappers that read/write the class field.

    The lowerer populates impl_method_renames (keyed by
    `class_name.method_name`) to rewrite high-level interface method
    calls to their mangled concrete names at every call site. The
    mangled name follows the pattern `InterfaceName_methodName`.

    For generic interfaces, subst_impl_type performs type argument
    substitution, replacing interface type parameters with the
    concrete types specified in the impl block.
    """

  invariant "TyInterfaceRef Emission and member_name Preservation":
    procedural
    """
    Interface-typed expressions lower to LType with kind=TyInterfaceRef.
    There are two source paths in lower_type():

    1. QualifiedType(base, member) — emitted by the checker for
       multi-param interface member references (e.g. DirectedGraph.N).
       This path PRESERVES member_name on the LType, which is required
       for the backend to generate correct per-family-member vtable
       dispatch.

    2. lower_named_type bare-name lookup — for single-param interfaces
       (e.g. `Hashable`), the interface name alone resolves to
       TyInterfaceRef with no member_name (erased fat pointer).

    Multi-param interfaces referenced by bare name fall through to
    TyAny, forcing callers to use qualified syntax.

    Additionally, dotted-string names (e.g. "DirectedGraph.N" from
    stringified qualified references) are caught by the dot_pos split
    at the bottom of lower_named_type and produce TyInterfaceRef with
    member_name set.
    """

  invariant "InterfaceType Dead Code Path (Phase 3 Prep)":
    procedural
    """
    The InterfaceType(iface_name, member_name, brand) branch in
    lower_type() is currently DEAD CODE — no checker pass or AST
    producer emits InterfaceType nodes today. The branch maps to
    TyInterfaceRef but DROPS both member_name and brand fields.

    Phase 3 MUST fix this before wiring up any pass that produces
    InterfaceType AST nodes: the member_name must be preserved on
    the LType (as QualifiedType already does), and brand handling
    must be designed. Activating this path without the fix would
    silently lose interface family member identity, causing incorrect
    vtable lookups at runtime.
    """

  invariant "family_params and is_erased Population":
    procedural
    """
    lower_interface_decl populates two fields on LInterfaceDecl that
    drive downstream vtable/monomorphization decisions:

    - is_erased: true when the interface has NO field declarations
      (method-only). Field-bearing interfaces (is_erased=false) are
      monomorphized, not erased. Checked via id!.ifd.children() length.

    - family_params: the subset of type_params that appear as
      receiver_type on at least one method. These identify the "family
      members" in multi-param interfaces (e.g. in DirectedGraph<N, E>,
      both N and E if methods receive them). Populated by scanning
      method receiver_types and intersecting with the type param list.

    Single-param gate: lower_named_type uses a len(itp) <= 1 check
    to decide whether a bare interface name becomes TyInterfaceRef
    (single-param, erased) or TyAny (multi-param, requires qualified
    syntax). This is a TYPE LOWERING gate — separate from the
    family_params classification.

    Bootstrap compatibility: the per-family-member vtable code in
    lower_file uses len(iface.family_params) > 1, which evaluates to
    false when family_params is empty (old binary that doesn't populate
    it), keeping the existing single-vtable-per-interface behavior until
    the new binary is bootstrapped.
    """

  doc "Technical Debt":
    """
    Known areas for future improvement:

    - Monomorphization interleaving: some monomorphization logic is
      mixed into the lowering pass. A cleaner separation between
      initial lowering and specialization would improve
      maintainability.

    - Short-circuit complexity: manual variable allocation and
      assignment for && and ||. Could be simplified if LIR supported
      a native short-circuit expression kind.

    - Duplicate renames: impl_method_renames grows large in programs
      with many relations. Optimizing storage and lookup would
      improve compiler performance on large codebases.

    - Capture analysis: LSpawnData.captures is always empty. Proper
      capture analysis to identify outer-scope variables used in
      spawn blocks would enable more efficient closure handling.
    """

